  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence is increasing that impacts from a\nnearby Nor'easter will be limited this week.\n\nOverall not too much change in the weather pattern this week. With \nan \"Omega Block\" taking form, upper-level atmospheric features \ngenerally remain stuck in place. Current model guidance \ncontinues to depict ridging over the central US and a strong \nhigh building over the Great Lakes through the middle of this \nweek. Although some uncertainty remains in the exact track of \nthe Nor'easter, this pattern tends to keep the developing low \nfar enough off the Mid- Atlantic coast to nearly eliminate \nshower potential across our region through next week. Overall, \nany impacts will be tied to coastal communities along the \nChesapeake Bay where potential minor coastal flooding and gusty \nmarine winds remain possible concerns this week. As for these \nmarine and coastal flooding concerns see the corresponding \ndiscussions below. \n\nLate in the week, this Nor'easter is expected to pull away from the \ncoast giving way to another strong high pressure system over our \narea. Heading into next weekend, dry and seasonable conditions are \nexpected to persist.\n\nTemperatures this week remain steady with highs in the mid to \nupper 50s and overnight lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s.",
